---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, WAITLIST_CONSENT_VERSION } from '../consts';
const updated = 'September 2026';
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="What HaulQ collects, why, and how to have it removed.">
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-ink text-white">
    <div class="hq-stripes absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-20">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-slate-300">
        Short version: this covers two different things. On the website, the only thing we collect
        is what you type into the waitlist form. In the HaulQ Driver app, we share a truck's
        location and check-in times with the carrier that dispatched the load — that's the whole
        job the app does. Nothing on either is sold, rented or shared with advertisers.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-mute">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-10 px-5 py-16">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What the website collects</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        When you join the waitlist we store the email address you give us, and the optional details you
        choose to add: an MC or DOT number, your fleet size, your equipment type, and which products you
        said you were interested in. Alongside that we record the date and time you signed up, the page you
        signed up from, the country your request came from, and a version tag for the consent wording that
        was on screen at the time (currently <code class="rounded bg-wash px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">{WAITLIST_CONSENT_VERSION}</code>).
        That last part exists so we can always tell you exactly what you agreed to.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        The MC and DOT lookup runs against public FMCSA records. We do not require an account and we do not
        attach lookups to you. The load profit calculator runs entirely in your browser; those numbers are
        never sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What the HaulQ Driver app collects</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        HaulQ Driver is the app truck drivers use to check in at a stop — reporting arrival, loading and
        departure — without a phone call. While a load assigned to you is in transit, the app shares the
        truck's position with the carrier that dispatched it, and with a broker's tracking link if the
        carrier chooses to make one for that specific load. That only happens while the app is open and a
        load is actively in transit — HaulQ Driver does not track your location in the background, or
        outside of an active load.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Each check-in you report — arrival, loading start, loading end, departure, and the timestamp it
        happened — is recorded against that load. If your carrier has connected an ELD (electronic logging
        device) integration, position data can also arrive that way instead of, or alongside, the app.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        We don't ask for a name, email or account to use HaulQ Driver. You're identified by the check-in
        code your dispatcher gives you, which they may optionally link to a driver record they maintain —
        that record, and anything in it, is entered and controlled by your carrier, not collected through
        this app.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        The carrier that dispatched your load always sees your check-in times and position. If they
        generate a tracking link for a broker on that load, the broker sees the load's status, a
        city/state-level position and freshness — not an exact coordinate — and the same check-in
        timestamps, and nothing else about you. HERE Technologies processes stop coordinates on the
        carrier's behalf to calculate routes and estimated arrival times, and Render hosts the servers and
        database the app and its data live on.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Check-in and position records become part of that load's permanent record once written, the same
        way the rest of a load's history is kept — they are not deleted on a timer. If you want a copy of
        what's on file for you, or have a question about a specific carrier's records, start with that
        carrier; we act on their behalf for this data, not as a separate service you have an account with.
        You can also reach us directly at
        <a class="font-medium text-brand underline underline-offset-2" href="mailto:privacy@haulq.ai">privacy@haulq.ai</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What we do with it</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        We email you about {SITE.name}: a confirmation when you sign up, then a message as each product goes
        live. That is the whole purpose. If we ever want to send you something outside that scope, we will ask
        first rather than assume the waitlist covers it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Who else sees the website's data</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Two service providers, both acting on our behalf and neither permitted to use your data for their own
        purposes. <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Cloudflare</strong> hosts this site and stores the
        waitlist, and keeps standard request logs. <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Postmark</strong>
        delivers our email. We do not run advertising trackers, analytics scripts or third-party cookies on
        this site. HaulQ Driver's own processors are listed in the section above, since they're different
        from the website's.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">How long we keep it</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Waitlist entries are kept until you ask us to remove them, or until we retire the waitlist, whichever
        comes first. If you unsubscribe we keep a record of the unsubscribe itself, because that is what stops
        us from emailing you again by mistake.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Getting out</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Every marketing email we send has an unsubscribe link. You can also email
        <a class="font-medium text-brand underline underline-offset-2" href="mailto:privacy@haulq.ai">privacy@haulq.ai</a>
        and ask us to remove your entry, or to send you a copy of what we hold. We will do it, and we will not
        make you explain why.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Changes</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        If this policy changes in a way that affects what we do with your information, we will update the date
        at the top and, for anything material, tell the people already on the list.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
